-- title:
--   Separating Igusa branch through an end of the blow-up chart
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. A prime $q$ and a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ are fixed, together with a prime $\ell$ satisfying $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic $0$, $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and `hι` requires a ring homomorphism $L \to \mathbb{C}$ carrying $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$. By `hH₁`, the subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$. By `hK`, the intermediate field $K$ of $L((t))$ over $L$ is the one generated over $L$ by the image, under the coefficient embedding $\mathbb{Q}((t)) \to L((t))$, of the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79) of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$.
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a henselian discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, with $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ has Laurent expansion the coefficient image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, and $j \neq 0$. The element $\varpi$ generates $\mathfrak{m}_A$, and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   The chart algebra is $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$. A maximal ideal $y \subset C$ is fixed with $\varpi \in y$, supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   The Drinfeld chart hypothesis `hArig`. For every point $z$ of the two-chart integral model $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) (the pushout of the two affine charts over $\operatorname{Spec} A$), every element $\varpi_z$ of the stalk at $z$ equal to the germ of $\varpi$ pulled back from the base and lying in the maximal ideal of that stalk, and every point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ whose ideal satisfies the same supersingularity condition as $y$ in `hss`, there exist a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v - fu)$, subject to seven clauses, summarised here: (i) $e$ carries the germ of each $a \in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) every $\tau \in \operatorname{Aut}_L(K)$ which is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserves $C$; (iii) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$ then the restriction of such a $\tau$ to $C$ is the identity modulo $y'$; (iv) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ as above preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ such that $\theta$ intertwines $e$ composed with the germ map with the action of $\tau$ on $C$, fixes all constants, satisfies $\theta(X_{j}) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0, X_1$, and satisfies $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, $M \equiv c\gamma$ entrywise modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod \ell$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; (v) a branch-separation clause: if $P_1, P_2$ are primes of $S$, each missing at least one of the classes of $X_0, X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and each containing an element of the form $a_iX_0 + b_iX_1 + h_i$ with $h_i \in (X_0,X_1)^2$ and $a_ib_j$ with $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$, then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to the stalk along $e$ differ; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ missing at least one of the two variable classes, containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing an element $X_0 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ has germ in the contraction of $P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$; (vii) a Hasse-type clause at the series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18): this series lies in $K$ and in $C$, there is $a_0 \in A$ with the difference in $y'$, and there are $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_i \mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit of $W$ for all $a, b \in W$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, such that the germ of that series minus $a_0$ is carried by $e$ to the class of $h$.
--
--   The chosen crossing point and its chart. A point $z$ of $X$ with germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ in the maximal ideal of its stalk is fixed, together with a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ above $z$ satisfying the supersingularity condition `hss'` and with $y'$ corresponding to $y$ (`hy'y`). Data $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1$ of the above shape are fixed at $z$, and `hW₁` asserts for them the same seven clauses (i)–(vii), summarised above, with $S$ now the quotient $W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$.
--
--   The blow-up chart. By `hJ`, the ideal $J \subset C$ is the infimum of all ideals of the form $\tau^{-1}\big(\text{contraction along } e_1 \circ \text{germ of } (\,C(\sigma_1\varpi_t),\,X_0,\,X_1\,)S\big)$, as $\gamma$ runs over $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ over the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. By `hB`, the $A$-subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is obtained from $C$ by adjoining all $x \in K$ with $x\varpi_t \in J$. A valuation subring $W \subseteq K$ is fixed with $B \subseteq W$ (`hBW`), and: `hR1` states $C \le B$ and that every element of $K$ is a ratio of elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2` states that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and that $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) states that an element of $L$ lies in $W$ exactly when it lies in the image of $A$, that $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ if and only if $b \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at the elements outside $\mathfrak{m}_W$; `hEQ` (five clauses) states that for any $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel the elements of $\mathfrak{m}_W$, compatible with $A \to \operatorname{ResidueField} A$, and equivariant for the level automorphisms preserving $W$ through an element $(\,$[`ModularCurve.FullLevel.redQ q γ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L230)$, c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c \neq 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; that the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, preserve $B$; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ receives $\mathfrak{m}_W$ under some such automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; that an element of $B$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ after all such translations is divisible by $\varpi$; and that a level automorphism preserving $y$ preserves $W$.
--
--   Remaining data. `hjK` and `hjC` place [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) in $K$ and in $C$. An integer $m \ge 1$ is fixed with $\varpi^m = \varpi_t \cdot(\text{unit})$ (`hmt`). A finite set `ends` of subrings of $K$ is described by `hends`: a subring $O$ belongs to it exactly when there are $a \in J$ with nonzero image in $K$ and a maximal ideal $P$ of the algebra $B_a$ obtained from $C$ by adjoining all $x \in K$ with $xa \in J$, such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at the complement of $P$, every element of $y$ lying in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$, and $O$ does not contain $B$. One such $O$ is fixed, with $O \subseteq W$ (`hOW`), $O$ local (`hOloc`) and Noetherian (`hOnoe`), $C \subseteq O$ (`hCO`) and $\varpi \in O$ (`hϖO`). Finally elements $c_x, c_y \in O$, a unit $u$ of $O$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the $\mathfrak{m}_O$-adic completion of $O$ with the crossing model `UVCrossingModel` of the adic completion $\hat A$ at $\varpi^m$, namely $\hat A[[X_0,X_1]]/(X_0X_1 - C(\varpi^m))$, and units $\gamma_U, \gamma_V$ of that model are given, subject to: $c_xc_y = \varpi^mu$ (`hcxy`); $\iota$ carries elements of $A$ to the corresponding constants (`hιc`); $\iota(c_x) = \gamma_U \cdot$ `UVCrossingModel.U` and $\iota(c_y) = \gamma_V \cdot$ `UVCrossingModel.V` (`hιx`, `hιy`); $c_y \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ (`hcyW`) and $c_x \notin \mathfrak{m}_W$ (`hcxW`).
--
--   Conclusion. There exists a valuation subring $W_x$ of $K$ such that: (1) $O \subseteq W_x$; (2) $C \subseteq W_x$; (3) an element of $L$ lies in $W_x$ exactly when it lies in the image of $A$; (4) $W_x$ is a discrete valuation ring; (5) $\varpi \in W_x$ and $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ is generated by $\varpi$; (6) there is $t \in W_x$ such that for every polynomial $p \in A[T]$, if the value of $p$ (mapped to $L[T]$) at $t$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ then all coefficients of $p$ lie in $\mathfrak{m}_A$; (7) the same holds with [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) in place of $t$; (8) $c_x \in \mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$; (9) $c_y \notin \mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$; (10) an element of $C$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ lies in $y$; (11) some element of $y$ lies outside $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$; (12) for every $O' \in$ `ends` with $O' \neq O$ there is an element of $O'$ not in $W_x$; and (13) for every $O' \in$ `ends`, every $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, if $\tau(O') \subseteq W_x$ then $O' = O$.
--
--   The roles of $c_x$ and $c_y$ in clauses (8) and (9) are interchanged relative to the hypotheses `hcyW`, `hcxW` on $W$: the branch produced is the one transverse to $W$ at the crossing point, and by (10) and (11) its centre on $C$ is $y$ while it is not the branch attached to $y$ alone.
--
--   Throughout, ä level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$\" means [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions and $g$ of nonzero expansion, and every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficient image of the ratio of those expansions, the complex reading of $\tau x$ along any $\iota$ with $\iota\zeta = e^{2\pi i/q}$ multiplies the $q$-expansion of $g\,|_k\,\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ into that of $f\,|_k\,\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$ is the real matrix with rows $(\delta_{00}, \delta_{01}/q)$ and $(q\delta_{10}, \delta_{11})$.
--
--   This is the construction of the Igusa branch through a prescribed end of the blow-up of the supersingular chart: a discrete valuation of the function field $K$ centred at the supersingular point $y$, transverse to the given branch at the crossing point, separating the chosen end from the other ends and from all their $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$-translates. It is the guard-prime ($\ell \equiv 11 \bmod 12$, $\ell \mid M'$) variant of the construction, valid without restriction on $q$, and it feeds the assembly of the cyclic decomposition of the ends with the Hasse and common-chart data.
